.TH BOUT 1
.SH NAME
bout \- a duel against an opponent written in a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B games/bout
[
.B -l
|
.B -c
.I file
|
.B -t
|
.B -s
] [
.B -q
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Bout
is a duel you cannot win by being quicker, only by learning what the
other one does before they do it.
Every attack is wound up visibly first \- a
.I tell
\- and that wind-up is the only warning there is.
Guard the side they are telling and the blow costs almost nothing;
guard the other side and it costs everything it says it costs.
While they are recovering from a blow they are open, and that window is
when a counter lands hard.
.PP
Up and down guard high and low;
left and right step out of reach and back in;
.BR x ,
.B .
or space strikes.
Enter takes the next opponent after a win, or the same one again after a
loss.
.B q
or Escape quits.
.SS An opponent is a file
The opponents are why this game is in the tree.
.I Bout
reads
.B /lib/bouts
and fights whatever it finds there; there is no table of opponents
anywhere in the program, and nothing in it knows the name, the rhythm or
the tricks of any of them.
Adding an opponent is writing a file.
Where
.IR camber (1)
demonstrates authored levels, this demonstrates authored
.IR conduct ,
which is the part of a game that is usually hardest to get out of the
source.
.PP
A file is a header of
.I "KEY VALUE"
lines \-
.BR name ,
.B health
and
.B reach
\- then a line holding
.BR % ,
then states:
.IP
.EX
state NAME
	INSTRUCTION
	...
.EE
.PP
The instructions are:
.TP
.BI wait " N"
do nothing for
.I N
frames
.TP
.BI tell " high|low N"
wind up, visibly, for
.I N
frames
.TP
.BI strike " high|low D"
the blow;
.I D
damage if it is not guarded on that side
.TP
.BI recover " N"
open to a counter for
.I N
frames
.TP
.BI goto " STATE"
.TP
.BI hurt " STATE"
go to
.I STATE
the next time this one is struck
.PP
Lines beginning with
.B ;
are comments.
Execution runs down a state and round to its start again; an instruction
that takes frames holds there until they are up.
.PP
That is enough for the three that ship to be quite different from each
other.
.I Ironhand
is slow and tells for a long time.
.I Willow
tells for barely long enough and throws two high before a low, so
counting is worth as much as watching.
.I Crane
tells low and strikes high, once, early \- and then never again, so the
lesson only works the first time.
.SS Checking one
.B -c
.I file
reads a file and says what is wrong with it, with the line, the way a
compiler would:
.IP
.EX
% games/bout -c /tmp/mine.bout
/tmp/mine.bout:6: high or low, not sideways
/tmp/mine.bout:7: no state called opne
.EE
.PP
A file that is right prints what it describes instead.
.B -l
lists what is in
.B /lib/bouts
and says nothing else.
.PP
Every
.B goto
and every
.B hurt
is checked against the states that exist, because a typo there is a
fighter that stops moving in the middle of a bout, and that reads as the
game being broken rather than the file being wrong.
.SS Other options
.B -q
starts silent.
.B -t
runs the self test and needs no window.
.B -s
fights for a few seconds and writes the frame to
.B bout.raw
as 24-bit BGR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /lib/bouts/*.bout
the opponents
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/bout.art
the sheets, the font and the palettes, as tiles(2) commands
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/bout.chr
the 8x8 tiles of the hall
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/bout16.chr
one figure in seven poses; both duellists are this art
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/bout.map
the hall
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/games/bout.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR camber (1),
.IR spire (1),
.IR tiles (2)
.PP
.B /doc/games-plan.md
says why this game is in the tree and what the others demonstrate.
.SH BUGS
.B -t
checks that every file in the directory reads, that thirteen kinds of
wrong file are refused, that a right one is accepted, that the tell
comes before the blow, that guarding the told side survives where
guarding the other does not, and that each of the three can be beaten by
reading the tells and none of them by standing still.
It presses no key: the tables that turn a key into an action are covered
by nothing.
.PP
There is no way for a file to say anything about where the opponent
stands.
Distance is the player's alone, and an opponent cannot close, retreat or
refuse to be backed away from \- so standing out of reach is always
safe, and only the clock stops it being a strategy.
.PP
The figures are sixteen pixels tall, which is small for a game whose
whole content is reading somebody's posture.
The tell is drawn as a bar as well as a pose for that reason.
.PP
An opponent that never writes
.B recover
can never be hit hard, and nothing warns about it.
